# Copy to .env. One file in the project folder serves two things that run side
# by side from it, each with its own settings:
#
#   - `pnpm dev` (development) reads the plain names below: APP_ORIGIN, HOST,
#     PORT, DATA_DIR, BACKUP_DIR, RATES_FEED, VAPID_SUBJECT, PUSH_ALLOWED_HOSTS.
#     Its data is .data and .backups, with made-up (fixture) rates.
#   - `docker compose` (the family's app) reads only the names that start with
#     BB_, and compose.yaml maps them to the container's own settings. Its data
#     is ./data and ./backups, with the real rates.
#
# The two never share a value, so neither can start with the other's address.
# The container refuses to start, with a plain message, if BB_APP_ORIGIN is
# missing. No secret belongs here: the app makes its own keys in DATA_DIR/keys.

# --- `pnpm dev` only ----------------------------------------------------------

# Web addresses allowed to send changes; separate several with commas,
# e.g. http://127.0.0.1:5173,https://<host>.<tailnet>.ts.net:5443
# The first one is also the address Face ID is tied to; browsers refuse an IP
# address for it, so use http://localhost:5173 first to try Face ID in
# development.
APP_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:5173

HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000
DATA_DIR=../.data
BACKUP_DIR=../.backups

# fixture = made-up rates, no network; live = ECB, Bank of Russia, Coinbase.
# Left out, `pnpm dev` and the tests use fixture.
# RATES_FEED=fixture

# Contact the push services get with each notification: a mailto: or https:
# URL. Left out, the first https address in APP_ORIGIN is used, else a
# placeholder. The VAPID keys are made in DATA_DIR/keys on first start.
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
# Hosts a push may be sent to, separated by commas; `*.x` matches any host
# below x. Apple's push service is the only one the app is allowed (SEC-8);
# add another, e.g. fcm.googleapis.com for Chrome, only for development.
# PUSH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=*.push.apple.com

# --- `docker compose` only (the family's app) ---------------------------------

# Required. The app's https address from Tailscale Serve, e.g.
# https://<host>.<tailnet>.ts.net (no path, no port). It is the Face ID
# relying party and the push contact, and the app refuses changes sent from any
# other address. Never the development address above.
# BB_APP_ORIGIN=https://<host>.<tailnet>.ts.net

# The user and group the container runs as, so it can write ./data and
# ./backups and you can still move and delete what it makes. Your own ids:
#   printf 'BB_UID=%s\nBB_GID=%s\n' "$(id -u)" "$(id -g)" >> .env
# BB_UID=501
# BB_GID=20

# The port on the host, bound to 127.0.0.1 (Tailscale Serve forwards to it).
# Not PORT, which is the development server's. Default 8080.
# BB_HOST_PORT=8080

# Left out: the real feeds (ECB, Bank of Russia, Coinbase). `fixture` gives
# made-up rates, for trying the container offline.
# BB_RATES_FEED=fixture

# Left out: the https address above is the push contact. Set a mailto: or
# https: URL only if Apple's push service refuses it.
# BB_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com
# Hosts a push may be sent to; leave at the default, Apple's push service.
# BB_PUSH_ALLOWED_HOSTS=*.push.apple.com
